## Unity Inspector Best Practices

### Serialized Fields
- Use `[SerializeField]` for private fields that need Inspector access
- Make reference variables public for easy Inspector assignment
- Use `[Header("Section Name")]` to organize Inspector sections
- Use `[Range(min, max)]` for numeric constraints
- Use `[Tooltip("Description")]` for field explanations

### Inspector Organization
```csharp
[Header("Health Settings")]
[SerializeField] private float maxHealth = 100f;
[SerializeField] private float currentHealth;

[Header("Movement Settings")]
[Range(1f, 10f)]
[SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;

[Header("References")]
public Transform playerTransform;
public PlayerStats playerStats;
```

## System Integration

### Event System
- Use UnityEvents for Inspector-assignable events
- Use C# events for code-based event handling
- Avoid direct component references across systems

### Data Management
- Use ScriptableObjects for game data
- Implement proper save/load systems
- Use JSON or Unity's built-in serialization

### Scene Management
- Follow the scene flow: Lobby → Loading → Scene1 → Scene2 → Scene3
- Use proper scene transition management
- Implement loading screens for heavy operations

## Quality Assurance

### Testing Standards
- Test all character classes (Warrior, Assassin, Wizard)
- Verify all weapon types work correctly
- Test UI systems across all categories
- Validate save/load functionality

### Performance Standards
- Maintain 60 FPS target
- Use Unity Profiler to identify bottlenecks
- Optimize draw calls and batch operations
- Monitor memory usage and garbage collection
